{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython - <<'PY'\nimport pandas as pd, glob, json, os\nprint('HAPE schemas')\nfor p in sorted(glob.glob('datasets/human-ai-parallel-corpus/text_data/*.parquet')):\n d=pd.read_parquet(p)\n print(os.path.basename(p), d.shape, list(d.columns), d.dtypes.astype(str).to_dict())\n print(d.head(1).to_dict('records')[0])\nprint('\\\\nMAGE')\nfor p in ['datasets/MAGE/train.csv','datasets/MAGE/valid.csv','datasets/MAGE/test.csv']:\n d=pd.read_csv(p)\n print(p,d.shape,list(d.columns),d.dtypes.astype(str).to_dict(),d.head(1).to_dict('records'))\nprint('\\\\nRAID')\nd=pd.read_parquet('datasets/raid/train_sample.parquet'); print(d.shape,list(d.columns),d.head(2).to_dict('records'))\nPY\nprintf '\\\\n--- model access ---\\\\n'\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom huggingface_hub import model_info\nfor m in ['Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct','Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B']:\n i=model_info(m)\n print(m, i.sha, sum((s.size or 0) for s in i.siblings)/1e9, 'GB metadata')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npython - <<'PY'\nimport pandas as pd, glob, json, os\nprint('HAPE schemas')\nfor p in sorted(glob.glob('datasets/human-ai-parallel-corpus/text_data/*.parquet')):\n d=pd.read_parquet(p)\n print(os.path.basename(p), d.shape, list(d.columns), d.dtypes.astype(str).to_dict())\n print(d.head(1).to_dict('records')[0])\nprint('\\\\nMAGE')\nfor p in ['datasets/MAGE/train.csv','datasets/MAGE/valid.csv','datasets/MAGE/test.csv']:\n d=pd.read_csv(p)\n print(p,d.shape,list(d.columns),d.dtypes.astype(str).to_dict(),d.head(1).to_dict('records'))\nprint('\\\\nRAID')\nd=pd.read_parquet('datasets/raid/train_sample.parquet'); print(d.shape,list(d.columns),d.head(2).to_dict('records'))\nPY\nprintf '\\\\n--- model access ---\\\\n'\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom huggingface_hub import model_info\nfor m in ['Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct','Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B']:\n i=model_info(m)\n print(m, i.sha, sum((s.size or 0) for s in i.siblings)/1e9, 'GB metadata')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/is-there-a-sounds-like-ai-direction-cc6e-codex-gpt-5-6-sol\nHAPE schemas\nhape-text_gpt-4o-2024-08-06.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@gpt-4o-2024-08-06', 'text': 'Second, human behavior is inherently variable and unpredictable, making it difficult to anticipate every possible action or decision that might lead to an error or an accident. No safety management system, regardless of its sophistication, can entirely eliminate the risk of human error. This unpredictability highlights the importance of fostering a safety culture that encourages mindfulness, adaptability, and continuous learning among employees at all levels. Moreover, communication plays a crucial role in bridging the gap between safety management systems and behavioral approaches. Effective communication ensures that safety policies are not only understood but are also internalized by the workforce. This can be achieved through regular training sessions, workshops, and open forums where employees can voice their concerns and experiences. By promoting open dialogue, organizations create an environment where safety is a shared responsibility and not just the purview of management. To further enhance this integrated approach, organizations should invest in technology that supports safety management practices. Innovative tools such as wearable technology, predictive analytics, and real-time monitoring systems can provide valuable data that can predict and prevent safety incidents before they occur. These technological advancements can complement human oversight and offer a more holistic view of safety management. Additionally, leadership commitment to safety is paramount. Leaders must consistently demonstrate their commitment through actions, not just words. This includes allocating necessary resources, acknowledging the efforts of employees in maintaining a safe work environment, and making safety a core organizational value. When leadership prioritizes safety, it sets a standard that resonates throughout the organization, encouraging all employees to follow suit. Equally important is the need for an adaptive safety management system that evolves in response to new insights, technologies, and changing operational landscapes. A static system is likely to become obsolete, whereas a dynamic system that incorporates feedback and lessons learned can effectively adapt and respond to new challenges. In summary, managing safety performance in organizations requires a balanced integration of safety management systems and behavior-based systems. The human performance approach provides a comprehensive framework that addresses both the technical and human aspects of safety. By understanding and mitigating latent organizational weaknesses, fostering a safety-conscious culture, leveraging technology, ensuring effective communication, and demonstrating leadership commitment, organizations can create a resilient environment capable of preventing accidents and minimizing human error. The continuous evolution of safety management practices calls for ongoing research and collaboration between practitioners and scholars. By sharing experiences and best practices, the collective knowledge of what constitutes an effective safety management system can be expanded and refined. The journey towards safety excellence is ongoing and requires dedication, innovation, and a willingness to adapt to new challenges. Through a concerted effort combining various safety approaches, organizations can not only protect their most valuable asset-their people-but also enhance operational efficiency and productivity. Safety is not merely an obligation but an opportunity for organizational growth and sustainability, ultimately leading to a safer, more productive future for all.'}\nhape-text_gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18', 'text': 'Such operational impracticality leads to the recognition that it is neither feasible nor strategically sound to attempt to create a fail-safe environment devoid of any latent weaknesses or error precursors. Consequently, organizations must instead foster a culture of learning and adaptation that emphasizes identifying and addressing these vulnerabilities when they emerge. This developmental orientation aligns with the concepts of resilience engineering, which advocates for an understanding of how systems can continue to operate safely under varying conditions, and how they can recover from unexpected disturbances (Hollnagel, 2006). In this context, it becomes evident that a mere compliance-driven approach to safety management is inadequate. Organizations need to promote an adaptive safety culture that empowers employees at all levels to engage actively and constructively in safety practices. This entails ongoing training, open communication channels, and the encouragement of reporting near misses and unsafe conditions without fear of retribution. Safety should integrate intrinsically into daily operations rather than relegated to a checkbox exercise in compliance. To achieve this, leadership commitment is paramount. Leaders must set the tone regarding safety values within organizations, modeling behaviors that prioritize safety alongside operational efficiency. A strong commitment from management not only motivates employees but also reinforces their role as central to safety management. This aligns with the premise that fostering an engaged workforce can lead to a shared responsibility for safety; each employee becomes a critical component in identifying hazards and suggesting safety improvements. Furthermore, organizations can benefit from using an integrated safety performance measurement system that blends leading and lagging indicators. Leading indicators, which include proactive measures such as safety audits, training completion rates, and employee engagement scores, provide an early-warning system revealing potential weaknesses before they can manifest as incidents. In contrast, lagging indicators focus on past performance, reflecting breaches in safety measures through the analysis of accident rates and lost-time injuries. This holistic approach supports a more nuanced understanding of safety performance and guides strategic interventions. Incorporating technology into safety management presents new opportunities for enhancing human performance and mitigating risks. Tools such as predictive analytics, real-time monitoring systems, and mobile applications can serve to empower employees and streamline data collection processes. Through technology, organizations can synthesize vast amounts of safety data for actionable insights, thereby enhancing situational awareness and enabling more informed decision-making. Moreover, interdisciplinary collaboration plays a crucial role in evolving safety systems. By integrating knowledge from established disciplines such as ergonomics, psychology, and systems engineering, organizations can develop more sophisticated frameworks to tackle safety issues comprehensively. Such collaboration extends to regulatory bodies, industry groups, and academia, allowing for the cross-pollination of best practices and innovative safety solutions. In conclusion, a paradigm shift towards an integrated safety management approach that marries safety management systems with behavior-based methodologies serves to enhance overall safety performance. As organizations recognize that human error is a symptom of deeper systemic issues, they can move beyond traditional compliance metrics and embrace a culture of resilience. This evolution encapsulates a journey toward not only preventing accidents but fostering an environment where safety is the responsibility of everyone-where safety practices are seamlessly woven into the organizational fabric and reflect a commitment to continuous improvement in the face of ever-changing conditions. In such environments, safety is not just an outcome; it is a core organizational value that fortifies the very essence of operational excellence.'}\nhape-text_human-chunk-1.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@chunk_1', 'text': 'Organizations often adopt safety management system or behavior-based system approaches to managing their safety functions in an attempt to achieve performance excellence. Organizations typically prefer adopting one system versus the other probably due to both pragmatic (e.g., resource and implementation constraints) and philosophical reasons. Commonly adopted safety management system approaches are described in consensus standards such as OHSAS 18001:2007 (British Standards Institute, 2007), ANSI/AIHA Z10-2012 (American National Standards Institute, 2012), and the OSHA\\'s Voluntary Protection Program (VPP) (Occupational Safety and Health Administration, 2011). These consensus standards do not strongly emphasize the \"human element\" when developing and implementing the required processes and procedures described in these standards. Conversely, traditional behavior-based systems adopt observational methodologies where workers use a list of defined critical behaviors, observe workers for these behaviors, and provide feedback. However, more advanced behavior-based systems (e.g., antecedent-behavior-consequence systems; DO-IT systems) uncover and correct organizational barriers (i.e., management system deficiencies) that inhibit safe acts (and therefore accidents) from occurring. The reality is that aspects of both of these systems are probably needed to effectively manage safety performance in organizations. An argument can be made that the two types of systems described above are complementary and that their respective strengths can be merged into a more balanced and comprehensive system to managing safety and in preventing accidents from occurring (DeJoy, 2005). Both aspects are prominently featured in the human performance approach to safety management which attempts to understand and eliminate the causes of human error (and thus accidents or events) in the workplace from both safety management and behavioral systems perspectives (see Fig. 1). The human performance approach to safety management in organizations can be viewed as potentially spanning the rational, natural and open system organizational approaches as described by Scott (1981). In human performance theory, mission, goals, policies, processes and programs (i.e., safety management system components) have latent organizational weaknesses that could give rise to flawed defenses and error precursors within organizations (Reason, 1990, 1997). These error precursors are unfavorable conditions that increase the probability of human errors while performing specific actions. Common error traps include time pressure, mental pressure, fatigue, being new to the task, distractions, and overconfidence (U.S. Department of Energy, 2009). In the human performance system, human error is merely a symptom of some trouble deeper in the system (Dekker, 2006). It is probably inevitable that latent organizational weaknesses (and resulting flawed defenses and error precursors) will arise within a system of safety management practices for a number of practical reasons. First, safety management systems cannot plan for, control, and defend against all potential error-prone situations because in doing so work would need to be planned and controlled to such a high and constraining degree that it would be time-consuming, unworkable and uneconomical.'}\nhape-text_human-chunk-2.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@chunk_2', 'text': 'Second, safety management systems tend to be institutionalized through policies, plans, procedures, and processes and therefore are not easily and readily adaptable to the natural and inevitable variations occurring in work being conducted and the hazards being encountered. Lastly, humans, who are fallible, design and implement safety management systems. Therefore, the lifeblood of a safety management system is shared with the managers and workers who have birthed the system and have given it daily life (and sometimes death). From a behavioral perspective, workers bring their beliefs, values, and vision to the design and implementation of safety management systems and ultimately in performing work. In particular, the individual worker interfaces with the safety management system by participating or engaging (or by not participating or engaging) in the system. Motivation to participate can be affected by workers\\' beliefs and values, which can impact the workers\\' degree, quality and consistency of participation. When accidents occur, the behaviors of workers, who are at the \"sharp edge\" of accidents and who interface with both the hazards and the safety management system itself, are closely scrutinized during accident investigations. This inquiry focus most likely leads to the often quoted and misapplied statistic that the vast majority of all accidents are caused by unsafe acts (i.e., human behavior) (Seo, 2005). In the field of human performance improvement, there are a number of human performance tools that can be used to systematically reduce the chance of human error. These tools can be viewed as vehicles for providing mental and social skills that complement a worker\\'s technical skills to promote safe and efficient task performance, such as carving out time to think about work, in particular the critical steps of that work (Muschara, 2012). Some human performance improvement tools commonly used include conducting pre- and post-task briefings, performing peer-checking, and using self-checking approaches such as \"take-a-minute,\" STAR (Stop-Think-Act-Review), and \"stop and seek\" for workers who do not believe they have the appropriate knowledge to make decisions (Wachter and Yorio, 2013). These human performance tools are emphatically \"worker-centric\" in that they engage workers to have more situational awareness concerning their safety, error traps present, tasks to be performed, and conditions/surroundings. Worker engagement in safety may systematically act to reduce the probability of human errors from occurring by making workers more involved with and aware of their tasks/surroundings and associated risks, as well as error traps that could be present. Thus, increased levels of worker engagement in safety activities could possibly be related to increased safety performance as measured by standard safety outcomes (e.g., accident rates).'}\nhape-text_llama-3-70B-Instruct.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct', 'text': 'Furthermore, the complexity and dynamics of modern workplaces make it challenging to anticipate and prepare for all possible scenarios. Second, the human element, which is inherently fallible, is an integral part of the system, and even with the best training and intentions, humans can make mistakes. Third, the ever-changing nature of work processes, technology, and environmental factors means that new error-prone situations can emerge unexpectedly. Therefore, a human performance approach to safety management acknowledges that latent organizational weaknesses and error precursors are inevitable and instead focuses on identifying and mitigating them proactively. In this approach, safety management is not solely focused on punishing or blaming individuals for errors, but rather on understanding the underlying systemic factors that contribute to human error. This involves analyzing the organizational context, identifying vulnerabilities, and implementing corrective actions to strengthen defenses and reduce the likelihood of errors. By adopting a more holistic and systemic perspective, organizations can move beyond traditional blame-oriented approaches and create a culture of accountability, transparency, and continuous improvement. The human performance approach also recognizes that safety is not just a technical problem, but also a social and organizational one. It acknowledges that safety management is not just about implementing procedures and protocols, but also about understanding the social and cultural dynamics that influence behavior. This includes factors such as leadership, communication, training, and organizational culture, which can either support or hinder safety performance. In practice, the human performance approach involves a range of strategies and techniques, including risk assessments, incident investigations, and root cause analysis. These tools help organizations to identify and address latent organizational weaknesses, error precursors, and other systemic factors that contribute to human error. Additionally, human performance approaches often involve the use of performance indicators and metrics to measure safety performance and track progress over time. One of the key benefits of the human performance approach is that it can help organizations to move beyond compliance-based safety management and focus on achieving true excellence in safety performance. By understanding and addressing the underlying causes of human error, organizations can reduce the likelihood of accidents and injuries, improve productivity, and enhance their overall reputation and competitiveness. In conclusion, the human performance approach to safety management offers a more comprehensive and effective way of managing safety in organizations. By acknowledging the inevitability of latent organizational weaknesses and error precursors, and by focusing on understanding and mitigating them, organizations can create a safer and more reliable work environment. By adopting a more holistic and systemic perspective, organizations can move beyond traditional safety management approaches and achieve true excellence in safety performance.'}\nhape-text_llama-3-70B.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@Meta-Llama-3-70B', 'text': 'Second, even if this were possible, there would always be the possibility of unforeseen and unintended consequences of plans and controls. Third, it is impossible to predict every situation that might occur during operations. Therefore, the human performance approach recognizes that human error is inevitable and that the goal of safety management should be to reduce its likelihood and consequence severity. Thus, the human performance approach acknowledges the inevitability of human error and seeks to address it proactively through designing and maintaining effective safety management systems and also reactively through identifying and eliminating error precursors using behavioral systems methods. This paper describes how the human performance approach to safety management in organizations can be used to identify and correct error precursors and thus improve safety management system effectiveness.'}\nhape-text_llama-3-8B-Instruct.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct', 'text': 'This recognition of the limitations of safety management systems and the inevitability of human error highlights the need for a more comprehensive approach to safety management. The human performance approach, which acknowledges the role of organizational factors in shaping human behavior and decision-making, offers a promising framework for understanding and mitigating the causes of human error. One of the key advantages of the human performance approach is its focus on understanding the underlying causes of human error, rather than simply addressing the symptoms. By identifying and addressing the latent organizational weaknesses that give rise to error precursors, organizations can create a safer and more resilient work environment. This approach also recognizes that human error is not a personal failing, but rather a symptom of a deeper problem within the system. In addition to its theoretical advantages, the human performance approach has been shown to be effective in practice. For example, studies have demonstrated that organizations that adopt a human performance approach to safety management experience significant reductions in injury rates and other safety metrics (Hollnagel, 2007). Furthermore, the approach has been shown to be particularly effective in high-risk industries, such as healthcare and aviation, where human error can have devastating consequences (Reason, 1997). Despite its potential benefits, the human performance approach is not without its challenges. One of the main challenges is the need to shift the focus of safety management from a reactive, compliance-based approach to a proactive, prevention-focused approach. This requires a significant cultural shift within organizations, as well as a willingness to invest in training and resources. Another challenge is the need to develop effective methods for identifying and addressing latent organizational weaknesses. This requires a deep understanding of the underlying causes of human error, as well as the ability to design and implement effective interventions. This can be a complex and time-consuming process, requiring significant expertise and resources. Finally, the human performance approach requires a commitment to continuous improvement and learning. This means that organizations must be willing to regularly assess their safety management practices and make adjustments as needed. This can be a challenging and ongoing process, but it is essential for achieving and maintaining a safe and resilient work environment. In conclusion, the human performance approach to safety management offers a promising framework for understanding and mitigating the causes of human error. By acknowledging the role of organizational factors in shaping human behavior and decision-making, organizations can create a safer and more resilient work environment. While the approach presents some challenges, the potential benefits are significant, and the need for a more comprehensive approach to safety management is clear.'}\nhape-text_llama-3-8B.parquet (8290, 2) ['doc_id', 'text'] {'doc_id': 'str', 'text': 'str'}\n{'doc_id': 'acad_0001@Meta-Llama-3-8B', 'text': 'Second, even if the most effective safety management system were implemented perfectly, there is no guarantee that it would prevent every accident. Third, no matter how good the safety management system is, there is always the possibility that humans will make mistakes. Fourth, people and machines are imperfect. Fifth, any complex system has many interdependent parts, and small errors in one part may cause large errors elsewhere in the system. Sixth, safety management systems require humans to implement them, and humans are fallible. Finally, the safety management system itself can be a source of error and failure. Safety management systems have been widely used as a means to improve safety performance. While there is some evidence of improvement in safety performance through the application of safety management systems, there are also numerous examples of failures. This article reviews the literature on safety management systems and provides recommendations for improving the effectiveness of these systems. The purpose of this paper is to investigate the relationships between different dimensions of occupational safety management systems (OSMSs) and occupational health and safety (OHS) performance using data collected from 85 Chinese manufacturing companies. The results indicate that four OSMS dimensions (including safety climate, safety policy, safety leadership, and safety information) are positively related to OHS performance. Specifically, safety policy and safety leadership are found to exert direct effects on OHS performance, whereas safety climate and safety information are found to influence OHS performance indirectly through the mediation of safety leadership. In addition, safety leadership is shown to mediate the relationship between safety climate and OHS performance. Moreover, safety information is found to play a role in moderating the relationship between safety leadership and OHS performance. The findings of this study suggest that safety leadership plays a key role in promoting OHS performance in Chinese manufacturing companies. Safety information also contributes to the promotion of OHS performance by facilitating the development of positive safety leadership behaviors.'}\n\nMAGE\ndatasets/MAGE/train.csv (319071, 3) ['text', 'label', 'src'] {'text': 'str', 'label': 'int64', 'src': 'str'} [{'text': \"White girls very rarely date Asian men. Even in Asia white men are much more popular than Asian men. Even though an Asian guy may have a preference for white girls he doesn't have much of a chance with one so he should instead pursue a non-White girl. Asian guys are penalized by their looks because I don't see them as being significantly less social, athletic, intelligent, wealthy, etc. Facial looks are genetic and cannot be changed easily. Having a fit body helps but not nearly as much as for white or black guys. I wish it weren't true. EDIT: Basically I'm saying Asian men are at a significant disadvantage in the dating scene especially if they are attracted to white women, and most comments so far seem to be confirming that view. Hello, users of CMV! This is a footnote from your moderators. We'd just like to remind you of a couple of things. Firstly, please remember to [read through our rules] . If you see a comment that has broken one, it is more effective to report it than downvote it. Speaking of which, [downvotes don't change views] ! If you are thinking about submitting a CMV yourself, please have a look through our [popular topics wiki] first. Any questions or concerns? Feel free to [message us] . Happy CMVing!\", 'label': 1, 'src': 'cmv_human'}]\ndatasets/MAGE/valid.csv (56792, 3) ['text', 'label', 'src'] {'text': 'str', 'label': 'int64', 'src': 'str'} [{'text': 'In much the same way as drugs, prostitution, gambling, or other \"victimless\" crimes, dog fighting is going to happen irrespective of the law. Legalization would lead to increased tax revenue, less organized crime. and safer, more humane environments for all those involved. It would also end the practice of baiting game dogs with helpless puppies and the like. I understand that many would argue that the dogs are victims (as are drug addicts and prostitutes for that matter), but only in the sense that eating horse meat (which is illegal in many places) victimizes horses, or even horse racing, which is already legalized, taxed, and regulated in controlled environments. The fact is countless dogs are already being put to death in animal shelters, often at the taxpayers\\' expense. Revenue from dog fighting could alleviate this cost considerably, perhaps even allowing animals to be kept longer, increasing their chances of adoption. Highly aggressive dogs are unlikely to ever be adopted however (except perhaps by illegal dog-fighting fans currently), nor should they be. It would only be the aggressive dogs used in fights, as they\\'re clearly willing. That\\'s why they need to be kept in separate cages, otherwise they\\'d be mauling each other of their own volition. Dogs too old, young, sickly, or cowardly to fight could still be put down as usual since they would make for unentertaining match-ups and would be bad for gambling. Even the dogs that do fight would receive better medical treatment afterwards from veterinarians on hand, andor humane euthanasia rather than drowning, hanging, etc. You certainly wouldn\\'t get the likes of a Michael Vick doing a Pete Townshend impression with a terrier in place of a guitar. Hello, users of CMV! This is a footnote from your moderators. We\\'d just like to remind you of a couple of things. Firstly, please remember to [read through our rules] . If you see a comment that has broken one, it is more effective to report it than downvote it. Speaking of which, [downvotes don\\'t change views] ! If you are thinking about submitting a CMV yourself, please have a look through our [popular topics wiki] first. Any questions or concerns? Feel free to [message us] . Happy CMVing!', 'label': 1, 'src': 'cmv_human'}]\ndatasets/MAGE/test.csv (56819, 3) ['text', 'label', 'src'] {'text': 'str', 'label': 'int64', 'src': 'str'} [{'text': \"Little disclaimer: this deals with US laws and procedures so I apologize if anyone outside the US isn't able to contribute, except to tell me how great it is having this in their own country. Last Sunday John Oliver's show dealt with this subject and chastised the US for being only one of two developed countries in the world without maternity leave that is paid for by the taxpayers. Here is a link to watch it if you haven't seen it yet: Here is the gist of my viewpoint: In the US, when a person is terminated from a job, heshe is entitled to unemployment benefits for up to 99 weeks or until they are employed again. Workers and companies pay into this and, while many people end up taking more than they give, it is the system we have and it seems to work for the majority of people here. When a person leaves a job VOLUNTARILY, however, they are not entitled to unemployment benefits. If you quit a job to go to another job this is fine. If you are quitting to go'find' yourself in Australia for a year, you better have a financial plan because you can't live off other peoples' money for your vacation. This system also seems to work well for most people. In the US, abortion is Constitutionally protected under a person's right to privacy. The details of the laws vary from state to state but there is no place in the US where a person cannot legally and safely obtain an abortion during the first six months of pregnancy. My stance is that if having a child is a choice, it should be one the parents must prepare for financially before making - just like if they wanted to voluntarily take time off work for other reasons. We have safety nets for people who are caught in situations they can't control. This is not one of them. Being a parent requires the ability to plan and budget anyway, and it includes many expenses that people will absolutely incur that will not be covered by the hard work of others. It should start with planning to be off work for whatever time having the baby takes. I completely support peoples' positions being protected while they are off and, quite honestly, think that length of time should be extended from 12 weeks to 24 weeks. But I REALLY don't like the idea of taxpayer dollars being used to support ANYONE who voluntarily leaves work - no matter the reason. Change My View. EDIT: I am also against he government mandating that companies provide paid maternity leave on simple Constitutional grounds. In the US the Constitution prohibits the government from mandating this. But I would LOVE it if companies started doing it voluntarily and think it would be a huge positive for attracting top talent. But it should be between employers and employees to figure out compensation packages. Hello, users of CMV! This is a footnote from your moderators. We'd just like to remind you of a couple of things. Firstly, please remember to [read through our rules] . If you see a comment that has broken one, it is more effective to report it than downvote it. Speaking of which, [downvotes don't change views] ! If you are thinking about submitting a CMV yourself, please have a look through our [popular topics wiki] first. Any questions or concerns? Feel free to [message us] . Happy CMVing!\", 'label': 1, 'src': 'cmv_human'}]\n\nRAID\n(4800, 11) ['id', 'adv_source_id', 'source_id', 'model', 'decoding', 'repetition_penalty', 'attack', 'domain', 'title', 'prompt', 'generation'] [{'id': 'e5e058ce-be2b-459d-af36-32532aaba5ff', 'adv_source_id': 'e5e058ce-be2b-459d-af36-32532aaba5ff', 'source_id': 'e5e058ce-be2b-459d-af36-32532aaba5ff', 'model': 'human', 'decoding': nan, 'repetition_penalty': nan, 'attack': 'none', 'domain': 'abstracts', 'title': 'FUTURE-AI: Guiding Principles and Consensus Recommendations for Trustworthy Artificial Intelligence in Future Medical Imaging', 'prompt': nan, 'generation': \"The recent advancements in artificial intelligence (AI) combined with the\\nextensive amount of data generated by today's clinical systems, has led to the\\ndevelopment of imaging AI solutions across the whole value chain of medical\\nimaging, including image reconstruction, medical image segmentation,\\nimage-based diagnosis and treatment planning. Notwithstanding the successes and\\nfuture potential of AI in medical imaging, many stakeholders are concerned of\\nthe potential risks and ethical implications of imaging AI solutions, which are\\nperceived as complex, opaque, and difficult to comprehend, utilise, and trust\\nin critical clinical applications. Despite these concerns and risks, there are\\ncurrently no concrete guidelines and best practices for guiding future AI\\ndevelopments in medical imaging towards increased trust, safety and adoption.\\nTo bridge this gap, this paper introduces a careful selection of guiding\\nprinciples drawn from the accumulated experiences, consensus, and best\\npractices from five large European projects on AI in Health Imaging. These\\nguiding principles are named FUTURE-AI and its building blocks consist of (i)\\nFairness, (ii) Universality, (iii) Traceability, (iv) Usability, (v) Robustness\\nand (vi) Explainability. In a step-by-step approach, these guidelines are\\nfurther translated into a framework of concrete recommendations for specifying,\\ndeveloping, evaluating, and deploying technically, clinically and ethically\\ntrustworthy AI solutions into clinical practice.\"}, {'id': 'f95b107b-d176-4af5-90f7-4d0bb20caf93', 'adv_source_id': 'f95b107b-d176-4af5-90f7-4d0bb20caf93', 'source_id': 'f95b107b-d176-4af5-90f7-4d0bb20caf93', 'model': 'human', 'decoding': nan, 'repetition_penalty': nan, 'attack': 'none', 'domain': 'abstracts', 'title': 'EdgeFlow: Achieving Practical Interactive Segmentation with Edge-Guided Flow', 'prompt': nan, 'generation': 'High-quality training data play a key role in image segmentation tasks.\\nUsually, pixel-level annotations are expensive, laborious and time-consuming\\nfor the large volume of training data. To reduce labelling cost and improve\\nsegmentation quality, interactive segmentation methods have been proposed,\\nwhich provide the result with just a few clicks. However, their performance\\ndoes not meet the requirements of practical segmentation tasks in terms of\\nspeed and accuracy. In this work, we propose EdgeFlow, a novel architecture\\nthat fully utilizes interactive information of user clicks with edge-guided\\nflow. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance without any\\npost-processing or iterative optimization scheme. Comprehensive experiments on\\nbenchmarks also demonstrate the superiority of our method. In addition, with\\nthe proposed method, we develop an efficient interactive segmentation tool for\\npractical data annotation tasks. The source code and tool is avaliable at\\nhttps://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleSeg.'}]\n\n--- model access ---\nQwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct a09a35458c702b33eeacc393d103063234e8bc28 0.0 GB metadata\nQwen/Qwen2.5-7B d149729398750b98c0af14eb82c78cfe92750796 0.0 GB metadata\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
